<!--
    MDT'S "SELECT ONE OR MORE APPLICATIONS TO INSTALL" PANE. DESIGN 11.2 lists
    this page and HDT had never drawn it; an admin arriving from MDT expects a
    checked list here and nothing else.

    THE LIST IS HAND-AUTHORED, AS TaskSequence.xaml's IS. The real one comes
    from the share's Applications\ folder - one app.yaml per application - and
    reading it is a command that does not exist yet. The markup is what is being
    settled here, so the entries below are three plausible rows rather than an
    invented mechanism: a page that renders the right shape against a fixed list
    is exactly as useful for laying it out, and does not pretend the wiring is
    done.

    AN ItemsControl, NOT A ListBox, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE SCREEN. A
    ListBox would give every row a selection highlight on top of its checkbox,
    so a row could be blue and unticked at the same time - two states meaning
    two different things, one of which does nothing. What this page collects is
    the TICKS. Nothing here has a "selected" row, so nothing here draws one.

    Tag CARRIES THE ID, Content CARRIES THE NAME. The technician reads "7-Zip
    24.09"; the sequence needs 7Zip-24.09. Putting the id in Tag is the same
    trick TaskSequence.xaml uses with SelectedValuePath, and it means the
    harvest reads a folder name rather than parsing a label somebody may
    translate.

    THIS PAGE COLLECTS NOTHING YET, deliberately. wizard.yaml's collect shape
    reads ONE property off ONE control - Text, SelectedValue, Password or
    IsChecked - and "every ticked row, joined" is none of those. Rather than
    bend a single-value declaration into a multi-value one from the markup side,
    the page ships uncollected and the schema gains the shape it needs next. A
    page that quietly dropped what was ticked would be worse than one that
    plainly does not carry it yet.
-->
<Grid xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
      xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
    <Grid.RowDefinitions>
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        <RowDefinition Height="*" />
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
    </Grid.RowDefinitions>

    <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Style="{DynamicResource HDTHintText}" Margin="0,0,0,10"
               TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="620" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
               Text="Everything published on the deployment share. Tick what this machine gets - the task sequence installs them in the order they are listed here." />

    <!--
        THE BORDER IS THE FIELD, and it uses the theme's brushes rather than
        naming colours, so a page added later cannot drift a shade off the
        boxes beside it.
    -->
    <Border Grid.Row="1" Background="{DynamicResource HDTFooterBrush}"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource HDTBorderBrush}" BorderThickness="1">
        <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" Padding="10,8,10,8">
            <ItemsControl x:Name="HDTApplicationList">
                <CheckBox Tag="7Zip-24.09" Content="7-Zip 24.09" Margin="0,5" />
                <CheckBox Tag="VSCode-1.96" Content="Visual Studio Code 1.96" Margin="0,5" />
                <CheckBox Tag="Firefox-ESR-128" Content="Mozilla Firefox ESR 128" Margin="0,5" />
            </ItemsControl>
        </ScrollViewer>
    </Border>

    <!--
        WHAT AN EMPTY LIST MEANS, SAID ON THE SCREEN. Ticking nothing is a
        legitimate answer - a base image with no applications is most of what a
        lab deploys - and a technician who cannot tell "none selected" from
        "this page is broken" will go looking for the broken thing.
    -->
    <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Style="{DynamicResource HDTHintText}" Margin="0,10,0,0"
               TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="620" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
               Text="Tick nothing and the machine is deployed with no applications, which is a normal choice. Applications a rule already selected are installed whether or not they are ticked here." />
</Grid>
